{% extends 'layout.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}{{ article.title }}{% endblock %}

{# The summary off the article's row, unchanged. It is the description,
   the card's line on the hub and the lead below -- one sentence in one place,
   because three copies of it are three chances for them to stop agreeing. #}
{% block description %}{{ article.summary }}{% endblock %}

{# Paired with `'hero': true` below: this tells the layout not to draw the
   breadcrumb strip, because the band draws the trail itself. #}
{% block hero %}yes{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
{% embed 'partials/article.html.twig' with {
    'title': article.title,
    'icon': article.icon,
    'lead': article.summary,
    'hero': true
} %}
    {# Already-safe HTML, on the same footing as /about: View\Markdown escapes
       raw HTML in the source rather than passing it through and drops unsafe
       link schemes, so escaping again here would print tags instead of prose.
       The controller converts; this only places it. #}
    {% block body %}{{ article_html|raw }}{% endblock %}

    {% block note %}
        {% include 'partials/article-verdict.html.twig' %}

        {# When the answer last changed, in /about's words and /about's class.
           After the question and not before it: the reader's way out of this
           page is the thumbs, and a timestamp above them interrupts the prose
           on its way to the one thing here to do.

           Guarded like /about's, and safe to guard because a test watches the
           pairing rather than a crash: an `{% if %}` on its own would let a
           controller that stopped passing the date leave a page that still
           looked finished, so ArticleCatalogueTest drives the controller and
           asserts the line arrives. That is the better of the two mechanisms
           -- Twig reads an empty date as *now*, so the alternative to printing
           nothing here is printing today, which is a confident lie about how
           current an answer is. #}
        {% if updated_at|default(false) %}
            <p class="article__note">Last updated {{ updated_at|date('j F Y') }}.</p>
        {% endif %}
    {% endblock %}

    {# The rest of this article's own group. An article has no data of its own
       to link out with, so its siblings are what it offers -- and they are
       what keep each of these reachable from somewhere other than the footer.

       Drawn as the hub's own card and not as chips -- the same partial, so it
       is the same icon, heading and rows a reader met on the way here. Titled
       with the group rather than "More help", which is what makes it a
       continuation of the article instead of a pile of everything else. The
       group's sentence is left off: by the time somebody is at the foot of an
       article they have read the thing it describes.

       Absent entirely where there is nothing to list -- an article alone in
       its group, or one whose category row has gone. The controller returns
       null for both, so this draws no empty card.

       `default(false)` for the reason the date below the prose carries one:
       null is a real answer here and `{% if %}` reads it correctly, but an
       *undefined* variable throws under strict_variables, and the suites that
       render this page to look at the band should not have to know about
       siblings. What stops the controller quietly dropping the card is a test
       that drives it -- see ArticleCatalogueTest. #}
    {% block aside %}
        {% if siblings|default(false) %}
            <aside class="article__aside article__aside--group"
                   aria-labelledby="more-help-title">
                {% include 'partials/help-group.html.twig' with {
                    'group': siblings,
                    'heading_id': 'more-help-title',
                    'summary': false
                } only %}
            </aside>
        {% endif %}
    {% endblock %}
{% endembed %}
{% endblock %}
